# Backend Layered Architecture

Three layers, each with a single responsibility. Never skip a layer or reach across one.

```
Controller -> Service -> Repository / Integration -> Model
```

## Layer 1 - Presentation (`app/Http`)

- Controllers (`Http/Controllers/Api/V{n}`) only translate HTTP to a call: validate via a FormRequest, call exactly one service method, return a Resource.
- Controllers never contain business rules, never call Eloquent directly, never call an external client directly.
- FormRequests (`Http/Requests`) own all input validation and authorization.
- API Resources (`Http/Resources`) own response shaping only.
- Routes live under a versioned prefix (e.g. `v1`) so the contract can evolve.

## Layer 2 - Application (`app/Services`, `app/DataTransferObjects`, `app/Contracts`)

- Services hold business rules and orchestrate one use case per public method.
- Services depend only on interfaces from `app/Contracts`, injected via the constructor. Never depend on a concrete repository or a concrete third-party client.
- Services accept and return DTOs or models; never accept a `Request` or return a `Response`.
- Wrap multi-write operations in `DB::transaction`.
- DTOs (`app/DataTransferObjects`) are `readonly` classes with typed, promoted constructor properties. Use a DTO instead of passing arrays or the raw `Request` across a layer boundary.
- Contracts (`app/Contracts/Repositories`, `app/Contracts/Integrations`) are narrow interfaces: one interface per aggregate or per external capability, not one wide interface for everything.

## Layer 3 - Data Access (`app/Repositories`, `app/Infrastructure`, `app/Models`)

- Only this layer touches the database or the network.
- Repositories (`app/Repositories/Eloquent`) implement a `Contracts\Repositories` interface and own all query building. Centralize shared filters (e.g. visibility/validity rules) here or in a model scope so no caller can bypass them.
- Models keep relationships, casts, and query scopes only. No orchestration logic in a model.
- External adapters (`app/Infrastructure`) implement a `Contracts\Integrations` interface, wrap the third-party SDK/HTTP call, translate the response into a DTO, and throw a dedicated exception from `app/Exceptions` on failure. No other layer imports a third-party client directly.

## Wiring

- All interface-to-implementation bindings live in `app/Providers/DomainServiceProvider.php`, registered in `bootstrap/providers.php`. Do not bind in `AppServiceProvider` or inline in a controller.
- A new implementation (different storage backend, different provider) is a new class plus one binding line; it must never require editing a service.

## Testing conventions

- `tests/Unit`: test services against fakes of their injected interfaces; no database, no HTTP.
- `tests/Feature`: test controllers/routes end to end against the real database, binding a fake for any external integration interface.

## Naming conventions

- Repository interface: `{Aggregate}RepositoryInterface`; implementation: `Eloquent{Aggregate}Repository`.
- Integration interface: `{Capability}Interface` (e.g. `...GeneratorInterface`); implementation named after the concrete provider/transport.
- Service: `{FeatureArea}Service`.
- DTO: `{Aggregate}Data` for write payloads, `{Aggregate}SearchCriteria` (or similar) for query filters.
